{"qid":"Q414","names":["Argentine Republic"],"title":"Argentine Republic","endonym":{"native":"República Argentina","lang":"es"},"capsule":"The Argentine Republic, unified under the constitution following the Battle of Pavón (1861), became one of the wealthiest nations in the world by 1900 through massive European immigration and grain and beef exports, governed successively by the oligarchic Partido Autonomista Nacional and then, after the Sáenz Peña Law of 1912 introduced universal male suffrage, by the Radical Civic Union under Hipólito Yrigoyen. A coup in September 1930 ended the constitutional order and inaugurated a cycle of military intervention; the most transformative figure of the 20th century was Juan Perón, who served as president 1946–1955 and again 1973–1974, building a mass labor movement. Military rule from 1976–1983 brought the Dirty War in which approximately 30,000 people were killed or disappeared, ending after the humiliating defeat in the 1982 Falklands War; democracy has been sustained since 1983.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. National boundaries are well established by treaty; the dataset's attestation begins 1930 (the first coup). Patagonian and Andean frontiers with Chile were disputed and settled by arbitration in 1902; the Falkland Islands sovereignty dispute remains unresolved but does not affect mainland geometry.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Argentina","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Argentina"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Argentina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentina"}],"britannica":"place/Argentina","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Argentina